     "I have data that looks like this:<div><br></div><div><div>&gt; str(getLocations())</div><div>&#39;data.frame&#39;:<span style=\"white-space:pre\">\t</span>121 obs. of  6 variables:</div><div> $ country  : Factor w/ 30 levels &quot;AR&quot;,&quot;AT&quot;,&quot;AU&quot;,..: 1 2 3 3 4 5 6 6 6 6 ...</div><div> $ latitude : num  -34.6 48.2 -35.3 -35.3 -22.9 ...</div><div> $ longitude: num  -58.5 16.4 149.2 149.1 -43.2 ...</div><div> $ os       : Factor w/ 4 levels &quot;lnx&quot;,&quot;mac&quot;,&quot;mixed&quot;,..: 4 4 4 4 1 1 1 1 1 1 ...</div><div> $ nodes    : num  1 1 1 1 15 1 1 2 10 1 ...</div><div> $ count    : num  2 1 1 1 1 2 3 1 1 1 ...</div></div><div><br></div><div>This works fine:</div><div><br></div><div><div>&gt; ggplot(getLocations(), aes(longitude, latitude, size=count)) +</div><div>+        borders(colour=&#39;white&#39;, fill=&#39;#dddddd&#39;, interior=FALSE) +</div><div>+        geom_point(alpha=0.8) +</div><div>+        coord_map(ylim=c(-60, 70)) +</div><div>+        scale_size(to=c(1, 5)) +</div><div>+        labs(x=&#39;&#39;, y=&#39;&#39;) +</div><div>+        opts(title=&#39;Cluster Locations&#39;) +</div><div>+        theme_bw()</div></div><div><br></div><div>...but when I change &quot;count&quot; to &quot;nodes&quot;, it claims it hasn&#39;t heard of it:</div><div><br></div><div><div>&gt; ggplot(getLocations(), aes(longitude, latitude, size=nodes)) +</div><div>+        borders(colour=&#39;white&#39;, fill=&#39;#dddddd&#39;, interior=FALSE) +</div><div>+        geom_point(alpha=0.8) +</div><div>+        coord_map(ylim=c(-60, 70)) +</div><div>+        scale_size(to=c(1, 5)) +</div><div>+        labs(x=&#39;&#39;, y=&#39;&#39;) +</div><div>+        opts(title=&#39;Cluster Locations&#39;) +</div><div>+        theme_bw()</div><div>Error in eval(expr, envir, enclos) : object &#39;nodes&#39; not found</div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>...however, if I store the data from nodes into count, it&#39;s fine again.  This makes little sense to me.  Any idea what&#39;s going wrong?</div>"
